WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.583 --> 00:00:10.304
Mark's father parked his car.

2
00:00:10.324 --> 00:00:13.445
"There's a crowd forming around Old Faithful," he said.

3
00:00:13.465 --> 00:00:16.447
"The geyser is probably about to blow.

4
00:00:17.327 --> 00:00:17.847
Come on, Mark!

5
00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:20.689
If we hurry, we might get to see the eruption."

6
00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:27.472
Mark had never seen a geyser erupt, so he and his father had come to Yellowstone National Park

7
00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:27.992
for the day.

8
00:00:28.596 --> 00:00:34.938
The park was famous for its geysers and hot springs, especially the geyser known as Old Faithful.

9
00:00:35.618 --> 00:00:40.640
Mark's dad had told him that geysers are one of the most amazing things found in nature.

10
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.821
A crack in the ground is filled with hot water, with bubbles and steam rising to the surface.

11
00:00:47.482 --> 00:00:50.783
Then—often with little warning—it erupts.

12
00:00:51.443 --> 00:00:55.204
A giant burst of steam and water shoots high into the air.

13
00:00:56.964 --> 00:01:00.106
Mark and his father made it to the geyser just in time.

14
00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:03.027
"Old Faithful is about to blow!"

15
00:01:03.648 --> 00:01:04.868
a park ranger shouted.

16
00:01:05.969 --> 00:01:09.351
Mark felt a vibration coming from deep beneath the earth.

17
00:01:10.011 --> 00:01:16.275
There was a strange gurgling sound, and then, from a hole in the ground, steam and water shot

18
00:01:16.355 --> 00:01:18.576
up over forty meters into the air!

19
00:01:18.596 --> 00:01:22.258
"Whoa!" Mark cried.

20
00:01:23.178 --> 00:01:26.020
Soon the water flowed back down into the hole.

21
00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:30.971
To Mark, it sounded just like water gurgling down the drain of a sink.

22
00:01:32.472 --> 00:01:34.353
Mark had a question for the ranger.

23
00:01:34.373 --> 00:01:37.335
"Why is this geyser named Old Faithful?"

24
00:01:37.796 --> 00:01:38.356
he asked.

25
00:01:38.376 --> 00:01:42.939
"Most geysers are unpredictable," the ranger explained.

26
00:01:42.959 --> 00:01:46.662
"But Old Faithful's eruptions follow a predictable pattern.

27
00:01:47.602 --> 00:01:51.345
If it erupts for two minutes, then it erupts again in sixty minutes.

28
00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:55.808
If it erupts for five minutes, it erupts again in one hundred minutes."

29
00:01:56.908 --> 00:01:58.549
"I get it," said Mark.

30
00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:01.449
"Old Faithful always does the same thing!"

31
00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:05.130
"Exactly." The ranger grinned at Mark.

32
00:02:05.991 --> 00:02:09.972
Soon Mark and his father followed the park ranger to an exhibit.

33
00:02:10.592 --> 00:02:17.114
There, Mark learned that Yellowstone Park contains more than half of all the geysers and hot springs on

34
00:02:17.334 --> 00:02:19.774
earth. "What are hot springs?"

35
00:02:20.274 --> 00:02:21.395
Mark asked the ranger.

36
00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:23.675
"Are they different from geysers?"

37
00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:27.517
"Yes, they are different," the ranger replied.

38
00:02:27.537 --> 00:02:30.178
"They form because of earth's water table.

39
00:02:30.818 --> 00:02:35.520
When rain falls, some water sinks deep into the earth until it reaches a layer of rock.

40
00:02:36.141 --> 00:02:41.683
The water sits on top of the rock and forms what's called the water table—a layer of water.

41
00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:48.086
"In some places, such as Yellowstone Park, the hot core of the earth is near this water table.

42
00:02:48.687 --> 00:02:51.148
So the water gets heated to boiling hot temperatures.

43
00:02:51.964 --> 00:02:56.408
If there is a crack in the earth above the water, the water expands and forces its way

44
00:02:56.548 --> 00:02:59.091
up. Water then pools around the opening."

45
00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:00.492
"I see."

46
00:03:01.153 --> 00:03:01.873
Mark nodded.

47
00:03:01.893 --> 00:03:03.875
"Hot springs don't erupt.

48
00:03:04.496 --> 00:03:06.818
But then what makes geysers erupt?"

49
00:03:07.639 --> 00:03:09.600
"Well," the park ranger said.

50
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.224
"A hot spring's crack, or fissure, is shaped like a pipe.

51
00:03:13.804 --> 00:03:17.688
But a geyser's fissure is wider, forming a large cavern underground.

52
00:03:18.248 --> 00:03:22.711
Hot water and steam rise and fill the cavern while the water at the top of the cavern

53
00:03:22.751 --> 00:03:23.731
cools off quickly.

54
00:03:24.171 --> 00:03:27.673
This cool water acts like a heavy lid on a pot of boiling water.

55
00:03:28.154 --> 00:03:30.055
Hot pressure builds up in the cavern.

56
00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:35.778
Suddenly all the pressure is released at once, shooting water and steam into the air."

57
00:03:37.479 --> 00:03:41.301
Mark and his father stayed a while longer, exploring the exhibit.

58
00:03:42.362 --> 00:03:48.270
They found out that Iceland, a small country in Europe, also has a large number of geysers and

59
00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:48.950
hot springs.

60
00:03:49.711 --> 00:03:54.735
The people living there can even use the energy from geysers and hot springs to heat their homes

61
00:03:54.795 --> 00:03:55.996
and generate electricity!

62
00:03:56.016 --> 00:03:57.798
"Look, Dad!"

63
00:03:58.218 --> 00:04:00.981
Mark pointed to a picture of a giant mud bath.

64
00:04:01.001 --> 00:04:07.286
"Sometimes the hot spring mixes with soil to form mud baths like this one.

65
00:04:07.726 --> 00:04:09.628
I bet it feels good to lay in that!"

66
00:04:09.648 --> 00:04:14.372
"Many people find these mud baths soothing," replied Mark's dad.

67
00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.501
"Japan is famous for its relaxing hot springs.

68
00:04:18.361 --> 00:04:23.564
Some people even believe that mud baths and hot springs can cure diseases—or at least take away some

69
00:04:23.624 --> 00:04:24.324
aches and pains!"

70
00:04:25.504 --> 00:04:27.105
The ranger heard them talking.

71
00:04:27.125 --> 00:04:32.987
"In Yellowstone Park the hot springs' water is usually too hot to touch," he said.

72
00:04:33.008 --> 00:04:36.989
"So unfortunately, people can't bathe in the hot springs here.

73
00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:40.210
But there is something special about our hot springs.

74
00:04:41.091 --> 00:04:43.612
Some forms of bacteria and algae live in them.

75
00:04:44.596 --> 00:04:49.359
These life forms are different colors and give the hot springs an unusual and beautiful appearance."

76
00:04:50.981 --> 00:04:56.024
Later Mark and his father thanked the park ranger and headed back toward their car.

77
00:04:56.645 --> 00:04:57.905
It was time to go home.

78
00:04:57.925 --> 00:05:05.090
"Dad, I wish we could go to one of those hot springs where you can swim and relax,"

79
00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:06.131
Mark said.

80
00:05:06.151 --> 00:05:08.173
"That sounds like fun!"

81
00:05:08.193 --> 00:05:12.756
"I've heard there are hot springs like that in Canada," replied Mark's father.

82
00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.657
"You can sit in a hot spring high in the mountains.

83
00:05:16.318 --> 00:05:18.041
Would you like to go there on our next vacation?"

84
00:05:18.462 --> 00:05:19.003
he asked.

85
00:05:19.023 --> 00:05:21.668
"Yes!" Mark said, grinning.

86
00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:24.935
"That sounds cool er, I mean, hot!"
